@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — where the failed run goes
#
# One generalization, formed on 2026-09-10 after the external alchemy was
# opened and printed its own excusing clause as part of the recipe. The
# holding had carried the same form in five further transmissions, entered one
# at a time over months and never read against one another.
#

pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The failed run is assigned somewhere other than to the operation"@en, "Der misslungene Durchgang wird anderswohin zugewiesen als an die Operation"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission prescribes an operation whose success cannot be established apart from the operation itself, the same transmission supplies a condition under which a run that produced nothing counts as no failure of the operation, and it locates that condition in the operator, in the person operated on, in the material, or in a third party, rather than in an outcome that a further run could establish."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:JinchengYaocheng, pmc:Shenbushi, pmc:SanjiLiuxiong, pmc:FailureIsSulkinessOrLostPower, pmc:BengeFailureCauses, pmc:AmuletFailureIsAlwaysExcused, pmc:ShangqingShijieZhengbian, pmc:NeidanFeidaofuren, pmc:ClaviculaDefectusOperantis, pmc:YinshuKouchi, pmc:PdmCompulsioNonAdventus, pmc:ShintoFutomaniCausaDefectus, pmc:ShintoSujinIteratio, pmc:AgrippaCulpaLectoris, pmc:QuimbyCulpaOperantis, pmc:CmBinglaiFuchai, pmc:CmLibuweishi, pmc:TurbaCulpaInOperante, pmc:TurbaFestinantiaCausaDefectus ;

    skos:note "Two witnesses added on 2026-09-23 from the Celestial Masters, and they are worth having separately because they put the empty run in two of the four named places within one transmission. At pmc:CmBinglaiFuchai the admonitions instruct that where a libationer treats an illness and it clears and then returns, this person is a bad person and is not to be treated further: the run is assigned to the person operated on, and the assignment is also the mechanism by which the case leaves the series before it can relapse twice. At pmc:CmLibuweishi the code assigns it to the operator, in the fullest failure protocol the holding carries — the officers do not serve, the pneuma does not descend, the interrogation grows daily more pressing, the sick person turns weaker and heavier by the day, and in the end there is frailty and no proof of effect — with the cause in the officiant's competence, which the same authority assesses."@en ;
    skos:note "The transmission supplies a third thing the fifteen earlier witnesses do not, and it is worth recording here rather than at either node. It penalises the report. At pmc:CmYanhuiWuyi one who meets calamity and says that serving the Dao is of no benefit thereby commits an offence of three thousand. The statement above is about where an empty run is assigned; this is one step further out, at the practitioner's testimony about the empty run, and it is stated in the operative text rather than in a gloss. A later run looking for a name for that construction should note that the same corpus also describes, at pmc:CmLibuweishi, what those who conclude that the thing does not work go on to do — burn their registers and wreck their chambers — and treats that conclusion as their further error."@en ;

    skos:note "Fifteenth witness, added on 2026-09-22 from the American mental healing of the eighteen-sixties, and it is the only one that costs the person who states it anything. The four places the statement names are the operator, the person operated on, the material and a third party; thirteen of the fourteen earlier witnesses use one of the last three, or an operator understood as some other operator, whoever he may be. This one names the operator who is speaking. Of the mesmeric years: if a failure occurred, the fault was the operator's and not that of the subject. Of the healing: I plead his case, and if I get the verdict the criminal is set at liberty; if I fail, I lose the case."@en ;
    skos:note "What the fifteenth witness adds is a distinction the statement can carry and did not yet display. The doctrine here is immunised exactly as the others are — a lost case says nothing against the science — and the man is not. That the transmission whose entire content is that belief makes and unmakes disease never once puts a failed treatment down to the patient's want of belief was searched for and is recorded at pmp:NewThoughtPatientsBeliefBlamedSearch; the easiest immunising clause in this register was available to it and is not used. One case in the same volume runs the other way, and the excuse there is the patient's belief in a rival advertised remedy rather than his want of belief in the healer, which is a narrower door than any of the other fourteen opens."@en ;
    skos:note "Fourteenth witness, added on 2026-09-22 from Renaissance learned magic, and it is the first from a printed book with a named author writing for a public. The conclusion of Agrippa's three books says that if any, through his incredulity or dulness of intellect, does not obtain his desire, he should not impute the fault to the author or say that the author erred or lied, but accuse himself, who does not understand the writings. The two causes named are properties of whoever complains, neither can be cleared in advance, and the sentence stands at the end of some two hundred chapters, where whoever has worked through them and has nothing to show meets it and no other account. What the witness adds to the statement is that the construction does not need a lineage, an initiation or a secret: it works in print, addressed to strangers."@en ;
    skos:note "A twelfth and a thirteenth witness were added on 2026-09-18 from the Japanese chronicle of 720, and they were owed rather than found: both stood in the same run as the eleventh, in a strand whose branch could not touch this file. The debt is prima-materia#744 and it is settled here. What the two add is one thing the other eleven do not have. In the rite at the pillar of heaven the excusing clause is not asserted by anybody — the rite produces a leech, the heavenly gods are asked by the Greater Divination why, and the answer, that the woman spoke first, is itself the output of a second procedure whose outcome nobody can establish either. And in the reign of Sujin the emperor worships exactly as the oracle instructed him and it is without effect, whereupon he bathes, keeps abstinence, purifies the hall and asks whether his ceremonies are not yet complete; a dream then supersedes the first instruction without withdrawing it. In eleven witnesses the transmission supplies the excuse; in these two it divines it."@en ;
    skos:note "An eleventh witness was added on 2026-09-18 from the demotic magical manual of London and Leiden, and it is the most repetitive of them. The work takes up the empty session six times in six different wordings and never once lets it stand: the boy who does not see the light, obduracy, obstinacy with the god unseen, a god who delays so as not to come in, a light that has not come forth, a light slow to come within, and gods who are inside and give no answer are each met by a further and stronger form of address. Where it does name a cause it names purity, which it calls its chief matter twice, and the fright of the boy who looked away. Both lie in the two people in the room and neither is anything an onlooker could check. What it adds to the ten is the count: this is not one excusing clause in a doctrine but the standing shape of every recipe in a working manual."@en ;
    skos:note "A tenth witness was added on 2026-09-16 and it is the oldest of them by four centuries. A single slip of the Pulling Book from tomb 247 at Zhangjiashan carries the instruction in two clauses: on waking, knocking the teeth prevents tooth decay; if there is tooth decay, knock them more. The first clause offers an outcome any practitioner could look for in his own mouth and the second says what to do when it is not there, and what it says is to do more of the same. That is the construction of the assay of the fourth chapter of the Baopuzi in miniature, and the two are separated by some four hundred years. The witness is worth listing apart from the other nine for one reason: it was buried between 186 and 156 before this era and read in 1984, so nobody added the second clause later in answer to anyone. The immunising clause is not a defensive move made when critics appeared; it was already in the instructions."@en ;
    skos:note "An eighth witness was added on 2026-09-11 and it is the only one that states the rule instead of instancing it. The internal alchemy puts the practitioner's question — he has held to the work and seen no result — at the head of its chapter on the signs of verification, and answers it twice in four characters: it is not that the Way has failed the man. In its place it supplies two conditions, both in the operator, an unenlightened master and ignorance of the times. Where the other seven have to be read off a doctrine, this one is the doctrine, and it names the possibility it excludes."@en ;
    skos:note "A seventh witness was added on 2026-09-11 and it is the limiting case of the statement. Where the others supply a condition under which a run that produced nothing counts as no failure, the Zhen'gao supplies a rule of reading under which the outcome that would end the practitioner's participation altogether — his death — is classified as the attainment, and it does so for every state a corpse can be found in, the intact and the putrefied alike. The condition has there been moved from the run to the observation, which is one step further than any of the six."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "A bounding case, not a witness and not a refutation, entered on 2026-09-26 and named here because a statement with nineteen witnesses needs its vacuous cases recorded as much as its instances. The Yogavacara's Manual falls inside the antecedent — its success is an inner appearance that nobody but the practitioner can establish — and it supplies no condition of any kind under which a run that produced nothing counts as no failure. It does not supply the excuse; it never raises the case. There is no caution in it, no danger, no counterfeit and no conditional sentence, established by reading the whole of it and searching it term by term at pmp:BoranPericulumSearch and pmp:BoranTerminusSearch. The falsifier of this statement is not met, because it requires the transmission to name an outcome that would count against the operation and this one names none. The statement is therefore vacuously true of the most sign-rich manual in this holding, and that is a weaker thing than its witness count suggests."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence for the statement, put as a question the next protocol can take up. The wording presupposes that a transmission of this kind contemplates an empty run, and every one of the nineteen witnesses does. A transmission that does not contemplate it at all is a third arrangement beside supplying the excuse and letting the outcome count, and the protocol at examinations/where-the-empty-run-is-put.ttl already records two near-misses of a different shape — conduct without a rule, and a limit set before the operation. This is the third shape and it is the one closest to the statement's blind spot: silence is not an excuse, and the statement cannot tell the two apart."@en ;
    skos:note "Two witnesses added on 2026-09-26 from the Turba philosophorum, read complete at the wording, and the pair is worth more than the count because the second one closes off the escape the first leaves open. At Dictum 17 and Dictum 29 the work says blame no one save yourselves and, in the same breath, that a reader who remains ignorant has had the truth concealed from him by God because his mind was not faithful — the condition in the operator, twice over, with a divine cause standing behind it. At Dictum 39 the same device is stated of a man of whom the work expressly says he knew the elements as well as the speaker, so knowledge is closed off as the variable and what carries the failure is four states of his soul: sadness, ignorance in ruling, excessive eagerness and haste. A holding that only had the first kind could be told that the transmissions are charging ignorance, which is at least a fact about a man that somebody could check. The second kind is not checkable by anybody and the work constructs it that way."@en ;
    skos:note "A ninth witness was added on 2026-09-12 from the Clavicula Salomonis, and it is the limiting case of the statement rather than another instance of it. The others supply a condition under which an empty run counts as no failure; this one supplies the condition in advance and in general, for every operation in the book: an experiment performed with due observance of days, hours and requisite solemnities which is found unsuccessful must in some manner be false and defective, and the operator must assuredly have failed in some matter. The same clause stands in three chapters of the work. Where the other eight name a party or a circumstance to which a particular non-occurrence goes, this one names the operator for all of them at once and adds that the whole book depends on the chapter that says so."@en ;
    skos:note "What each witness supplies, in the transmission's own terms. The golden elixir: where the assay yields no gold the drug is to be sealed and fired again for the same count of days, and then it never fails to form; and where it still does not form, an unbeliever may have slandered it. The medical canon: where the treatment establishes nothing, the spirit does not lend itself, and the passage supplies no mark by which the absence could have been seen beforehand. The burial book: the commentary on the sixth adversity enumerates the ways in which nothing happens, and every one of them is a circumstance of the family rather than of the site. The Kongo corpus: a charged thing that fails is either sulky and wants a sacrifice or has lost its power and wants renewing, and both branches cost a fee. The Zande oracle: eight named causes for a self-contradiction, from the wrong variety of creeper to the anger of the ghosts, and the recorder's summary that the errors as well as the sound judgements are taken as proof of its accuracy. The Slave Coast amulet: where the thing fails at the single danger it was made against, they are never at a loss for an excuse."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is about where the failure is put and not about whether the operations work. A transmission that supplies such a clause is not thereby making a false claim; it is making one that the ordinary check cannot reach, because the check's negative outcome has been given a home in advance. That is the difference this project exists to record, and it is a property of the wording rather than of the world."@en ;
    skos:note "The witnesses are not of equal standing and the difference runs along one line. In the three Chinese cases the excusing clause is printed by the transmission itself, as part of the instruction or of the commentary that travels with it: nobody is being answered, and no outside observer is involved. In the three African cases it is the report of a European recorder, and two of the three recorders say so in their own voice — one holds that the practitioners themselves are responsible for the prohibitions because a prohibition leaves a reasonable excuse, and another summarises an attitude rather than quoting an informant. Observers of that period were looking for exactly this shape. The generalization therefore rests on the Chinese witnesses, where the clause is self-attested, and is corroborated with that caveat by the others."@en ;
    skos:note "Counter-search, carried against this holding before the statement was written, as SPEC §16 requires. Two near-misses and no refutation. The Sami material reports two practitioners who set a period, named in advance the sign they demanded, received nothing and destroyed the instrument without explaining the failure away; it does not refute, because it reports conduct and not a rule of the transmission, and the node itself flags that both cases stand in a missionary's chapter on conversion. The Zande material prescribes a corroborative second test whose contradiction voids the verdict, which is an outcome a further run can establish and looks at first like the counter-case; it does not refute either, because the same transmission carries the eight causes beside it, so that the internal check and the external excuse coexist. What that pair shows is worth more than a clean confirmation: a transmission can hold a real check and an unlimited excuse at once, and the check then bounds nothing."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission of the kind described whose earliest witness names an outcome that would count against the operation and supplies no condition assigning a failed run to the operator, the person operated on, the material or a third party. One such case refutes it. The likeliest places to look are the transmissions this holding carries that already name an observable sign of success, since a sign that can be present can also be absent: the feather at the nostril of the eighth chapter of the Baopuzi, the gold-coloured body of the golden liquor, and the ten-day term of the medical canon."@en ;
    skos:note "The form was described from the outside, at the same period as two of the witnesses, by a first-century critic whose account this holding carries at pmc:BiansuiDispute: he put a counter-example to a practitioner three times and was answered three times with a fresh condition. Seeing the same form printed as instruction at pmc:JinchengYaocheng is what made the generalization worth forming. The description from outside is not counted among the witnesses, because a critic reporting the move is not a transmission supplying it."@en .
